+/// Output-split TP of one dense GEMV over two cards, with no peer-to-peer access: the activation goes to
+/// device1 through `host_xq` and its half output comes back through `host_y1`, both mapped pinned memory,
+/// and each card's wait is a bounded spin on a mapped flag - the same hand-off the layer split uses for
+/// its residual (docs/MULTI_GPU.md: the split needs no NVLink and no P2P).  w0/w1 are the first/second
+/// halves of the rows (n_out/2 each, `native_mmvq_weight_bytes` / 2 apart in the same layout), xq0 the
+/// Q8_1 activation on device0, y0h device0's half output and y the assembled n_out-per-column output on
+/// device0, which is what the consumer reads.  Both halves are assembled because the consumer wants one
+/// contiguous row of n_out floats per column.
+///
+/// Ordering, and why it cannot stall: device0 raises `go` only after its publish kernel has put the
+/// activation in host memory (a system fence inside the publish, ahead of the flag), and device1 raises
+/// `done` only after its publish has put the half output in host memory.  Neither card waits for a flag the
+/// other card raises later in the same stream than the work waiting for it - device0 raises `go` before it
+/// spins on `done` - so there is no cycle, no cross-device event, no host sync inside the window, and a
+/// lost publish ends the window through `err` instead of hanging a card.  The two streams are driven by
+/// separate graphs, one per card; a window's peer half is captured on `stream1` beside its own.
